This directory contains some reconstructions against which to benchmark the model simulations.

midHolocene

* Bartlein_mat.csv relates to the Bartlein et al (2011) pollen reconstructions. The data has been extracted into a csv in this repo: https://github.com/chrisbrierley/PMIP4-midHolocene/blob/master/recons/Bartlein/mat.csv
* temp12k_anom6k_mat.csv relates to the extracted data from the temp12k database that is described in Brierley et al (2020). It can be
found at https://github.com/chrisbrierley/PMIP4-midHolocene/blob/master/recons/anom6k/anom6k_annual_grid_ave.csv
* The paleodata assimilation product by Erb et al (2022). It was downloaded from https://zenodo.org/records/6426332/files/holocene_reconstruction.nc and averaged between 5.5-6.5ka.
  IMPORTANT: the published recon_tas_mean field is referenced to 3-5ka, not to the preindustrial - the paper states "The reference period of each reconstruction is 3-5 ka"
  (Fig 13 caption), and Erb et al themselves re-reference when quoting a mid-Holocene number ("0.09 degC relative to the past millennium"). So Erbetal2022_6ka_reconstruction.nc
  is NOT comparable with a model midHolocene-piControl anomaly; taken at face value it would make every model look far too warm. Erbetal2022_6ka_minus_0-1ka_anom.nc is what
  the carpet diagram actually uses: the 5.5-6.5ka mean minus the 0-1ka mean, so the shared 3-5ka baseline cancels and what is left is a 6ka-(0-1ka) anomaly, on the same
  footing as the Osman LGM field. It was made with:
      ncks -v recon_tas_mean holocene_reconstruction.nc Erbetl2022_holocene_reconstruction.nc
      ncwa -a ages -d ages,5500.,6500. Erbetl2022_holocene_reconstruction.nc Erbetal2022_6ka_reconstruction.nc
      ncwa -a ages -d ages,0.,1000.   Erbetl2022_holocene_reconstruction.nc Erbetal2022_0-1ka_reconstruction.nc
      ncdiff Erbetal2022_6ka_reconstruction.nc Erbetal2022_0-1ka_reconstruction.nc Erbetal2022_6ka_minus_0-1ka_anom.nc
  It is a gridded 64x96 field, so it is flattened to points and given cos(latitude) weights for an area-fair RMSE.
* P2F-recommended-SST.midHolocene.xls is the upcoming dataset from the the Past 2 Future project. It contains ocean temperature reconstructions. It is a work in progress and is compiled by Anna Cutmore (Durham). Only the recommded values should be used. 

Last Glacial Maximum
* Bartlein_mat_21ka.csv is the 21ka half of the Bartlein et al (2011) pollen synthesis - the same product as the midHolocene Bartlein file, just the other time slice. Downloaded
  from https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/pub/data/paleo/pollen/recons/bartlein2010/mat_delta_21ka_ALL_grid_2x2.csv (checked: the 06ka file from the same directory is identical to
  Bartlein_mat.csv on all 635 shared cells, so it is the same source). 98 cells, anomaly column mat_anm_mean, scattered so unit weights. Note the two Alaskan cells at 67N give
  +13.4 and +8.1 degC, the known anomalously-warm Beringian pollen reconstructions - they are kept, not screened.
* LGM_foram_reconstruction.tab is Jonkers, Laepple, Rillo, Shi, Dolman, Lohmann, Paul, Mix & Kucera (2023), "Last Glacial Maximum seawater temperature estimates based on planktonic
  foraminifera", PANGAEA, https://doi.org/10.1594/PANGAEA.962957 (bundled publication https://doi.org/10.1594/PANGAEA.962958). Temperatures are estimated with the Modern Analogue
  Technique against the ForCenS core-top synthesis and World Ocean Atlas 1998, taking the weighted average of the 10 closest analogues - so this is a transfer-function PROXY
  reconstruction, not a data assimilation product. In the carpet diagram it sits in the middle group, below the first double rule, with the other post-2021 reconstruction datasets
  (the two P2F rows) rather than with the older proxy compilations above or the DA products below. 647 sites, no missing values, scattered so unit weights.
  The anomaly column is "T anomaly [degC]", already the estimate minus the World Ocean Atlas climatology at the same site, so it needs no re-referencing.
  Two things to watch. (1) PANGAEA tab-delimited format: ~663 lines of metadata - citation, abstract, a per-core event list, parameter definitions - precede a line containing just
  */, and the tab-separated column header is the line after that. Step 1 locates the */ marker rather than hard-coding a line count, because the header length changes between
  PANGAEA exports. (2) These are seawater temperatures at 50 m depth, annual mean - NOT sea-surface temperature, and not surface air temperature. The carpet diagram compares them
  against model tas, which is a longer reach than the SST rows (P2F, Hoffman, Foley-Dowsett) that are already an approximation. Worth remembering before reading much into the row.
  Duplicated locations: 647 rows but only 640 distinct coordinate pairs and 639 distinct Events. Three patterns, all kept as published rather than de-duplicated.
  (a) Sub-sample splits of one core, 4 pairs, flagged by a parenthesised letter on Site - R657 (S)/(W), RC09-150 (P)/(W), V28-342 (P)/(W), RC12-113 (A)/(M). These 8 rows are the
      only ones in the file carrying such a suffix and they form exactly 4 pairs. R657's two rows are numerically identical; the others differ by 0.15-0.89 degC.
  (b) Two genuinely different cores at one published position: V29-172 and V29-173, distinct Vema cores (water depths -3257 m and -3278 m, recoveries 5.03 m and 0.57 m) both
      listed at 33.700N 29.383W. Not an error, just a coarse position.
  (c) The same core entered twice from different source compilations: V17-165 (LGM_0069, 4 samples, +/-0.80 vs LGM_0413, 1 sample, +/-1.60), RC11-86 (LGM_0170, 2 samples vs
      LGM_0510, 1 sample), GIK12392-1/M12392 (the header confirms these are one core, "GIK12392-1 (M12392-1)"; coordinates differ by 0.005 deg, anomalies -4.15 vs -5.28 degC),
      and RC14-39.
  KNOWN ERROR, not corrected: RC14-39 appears at latitude -5.857 (LGM_0291) and +5.850 (LGM_0520) on the same longitude. The file's own event header gives LATITUDE: -5.857000,
  so LGM_0520 has a flipped sign and is placed 11.7 deg away in the wrong hemisphere. Same class of problem as the P2F GeoB7112-5 core, which Step 1 does fix. It is left alone
  here because the cost is negligible - see below - but fix it if this row ever carries weight.
  Impact of all of the above: de-duplicating to 640 points moves every model's Jonkers RMSE by at most 0.031 degC, and the sign-flipped point is 1 of 647 and sits in the Indian
  Ocean, outside both current region boxes. So the duplicates are harmless at present; they would matter more in a regional subset that happened to contain them.
* The Cleator et al (2020) reconstruction is a data assimilation product, NOT a proxy compilation - checked, and its own preamble says so: "made by combining pollen based
  reconstructions (from Bartlein et al. 2011) and averaged outputs of LGM simulations from ... PMIP ... under a variational data assimilation technique". The Reading record
  holds only LGM_reconstruction.csv and a README, with no proxy-only file, and the data sit on a regular 2x2 grid rather than at sites. So its proxy input is the Bartlein 21ka
  file above, which means the two rows are not independent. It was downloaded from https://researchdata.reading.ac.uk/244/1/LGM_reconstruction.csv and only the MAT column is
  relevant. In the carpet diagram it sits below the double rule with the other DA products.
* Annan_etal22.SAT.mean.lgm.nc is the data assimilation product of Annan, Hargreaves & Mauritsen (2022, Clim Past 18, 1883, https://cp.copernicus.org/articles/18/1883/2022/), which
  blends proxies with a 19-member PMIP prior using an ensemble Kalman filter. The SAT.mean field is already an LGM anomaly and needs no re-referencing: its area-weighted global mean
  is -4.46 degC, against the -4.5 +/- 0.9 degC the paper reports. Two things to watch if you touch this file - the coordinates are called latitude/longitude rather than lat/lon, and the
  longitude axis is rolled (it runs 181..359 then 1..179 rather than ascending). The roll is harmless as used, because Step 1 flattens the grid to points so each value keeps its own
  longitude and the field is never interpolated; it would matter if anything tried to interpolate it. 2x2 degree grid, 16200 points, all finite, cos(latitude) weights.
* The Osman file is the last Glacial Maximum Reanalysis from Osman et al (2020). It is averaged between the 20900 and 21100 bins. Downloaded from https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/pub/data/paleo/reconstructions/osman2021/LGMR_SAT_climo.nc
* P2F-Recommended-SST-records_LGM_C.Brierley.xls is the upcoming dataset from the the Past 2 Future project. It contains ocean temperature reconstructions. It is a work in progress and is compiled by Anna Cutmore (Durham). Only the recommded values should be used. 


last interglacial (lig127k)
* The Hoffman et al (2017) and Capron et al (2017) reconstruction compilations are taken from the Supplement of Otto-Bleisner et al (2020) from https://cp.copernicus.org/articles/17/63/2021/cp-17-63-2021-supplement.zip

midPliocene-eoi400
* The data from the Foley & Dowestt (2019). It was published in Haywood et al (2020), who used the NOAA ERSST anomalies. Downloaded from https://cp.copernicus.org/articles/16/2095/2020/cp-16-2095-2020-supplement.zip
* PlioDA, the data assimilation product of Tierney et al (2024, AGU Advances, doi:10.1029/2024AV001356), in plioDAMainResults.nc. Its time axis is in Ma and holds three
  slices: 0 (modern), 3.25 and 4.75. The 3.25Ma slice is the mid-Piacenzian, which is what midPliocene-eoi400 targets, so the anomaly is the 3.25Ma slice minus the modern
  one. Unlike the Erb file this needs no re-referencing, because both slices come from the same product. Made with:
      ncwa -O -v tas_annual -d time,1 -a time plioDAMainResults.nc plioDAMainResults.tas_annual_latePlio.nc
      ncwa -O -v tas_annual -d time,0 -a time plioDAMainResults.nc plioDAMainResults.tas_annual_modern.nc
      ncdiff plioDAMainResults.tas_annual_latePlio.nc plioDAMainResults.tas_annual_modern.nc plioDAMainResults.tas_annual_latePlio_changes.nc
  The carpet diagram reads the _changes file. It is a gridded 180x360 field (the 3 southernmost and 3 northernmost latitude rows are all fill and get dropped, leaving
  62640 points), flattened to points with cos(latitude) weights. Global mean warming is +5.3 degC.


P2F recommended SST records (midHolocene and lgm rows)
------------------------------------------------------
recons/midHolocene/P2F-recommended-SST.midHolocene.xlsx and
recons/lgm/P2F-Recommended-SST-records_LGM_C.Brierley.xlsx are the supplied
workbooks. Step 1 reads a verbatim CSV dump of each rather than the .xlsx
itself, because the my-cli-py environment has no openpyxl. The dumps are
P2F_recommended_sst_midHolocene.csv and P2F_recommended_sst_lgm.csv, made with
the esmvaltool environment (which does have openpyxl):

  python - <<'PY'
  import pandas as pd
  for src, sheet, dst in [
      ('recons/lgm/P2F-Recommended-SST-records_LGM_C.Brierley.xlsx', 'LGM',
       'recons/lgm/P2F_recommended_sst_lgm.csv'),
      ('recons/midHolocene/P2F-recommended-SST.midHolocene.xlsx', 'MH',
       'recons/midHolocene/P2F_recommended_sst_midHolocene.csv')]:
      pd.read_excel(src, sheet_name=sheet).to_csv(dst, index=False)
  PY

No columns are renamed or dropped in the dump - all of the normalising happens
in Step 1. The anomaly used is the SST_Anomaly column, which is the core's
annual SST minus the ERSSTv6 1870-1899 mean at its grid cell. Install openpyxl
into my-cli-py if you would rather Step 1 read the .xlsx directly.
